"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,365\nEpisode 7\n\n3\n00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,177\nStorytelling\n\n4\n00:00:11,977 --> 00:00:14,202\nHere are ten unrelated words.\n\n5\n00:00:14,543 --> 00:00:16,845\nNow imagine twelve lists like this,\n\n6\n00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:18,237\none after another.\n\n7\n00:00:18,704 --> 00:00:19,978\nIn a classic study,\n\n8\n00:00:20,147 --> 00:00:25,344\npeople who simply rehearsed each list later recalled about thirteen percent of the words.\n\n9\n00:00:25,729 --> 00:00:30,331\nPeople who wove each list into a little story recalled ninety-three percent.\n\n10\n00:00:30,501 --> 00:00:33,533\nA story is a structure the brain already has.\n\n11\n00:00:33,814 --> 00:00:35,099\nSomeone wants something.\n\n12\n00:00:35,443 --> 00:00:36,889\nSomething gets in the way.\n\n13\n00:00:37,292 --> 00:00:39,162\nOne event causes the next.\n\n14\n00:00:39,509 --> 00:00:41,833\nEach piece hooks onto the one before it,\n\n15\n00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:44,762\nso the whole thing comes back as a single chunk.\n\n16\n00:00:44,932 --> 00:00:46,176\nIt holds for text,\n\n17\n00:00:46,195 --> 00:00:46,713\ntoo.\n\n18\n00:00:47,147 --> 00:00:50,603\nA meta-analysis pooled more than seventy-five samples,\n\n19\n00:00:50,811 --> 00:00:53,299\nand over thirty-three thousand readers.\n\n20\n00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:58,245\nStories were understood and remembered better than expository essays.\n\n21\n00:00:58,415 --> 00:01:00,286\nBrain imaging hints at why.\n\n22\n00:01:00,741 --> 00:01:02,841\nPeople listened to a real story,\n\n23\n00:01:03,048 --> 00:01:05,093\nsometimes scrambled by words,\n\n24\n00:01:05,094 --> 00:01:07,739\nby sentences, or by paragraphs.\n\n25\n00:01:08,219 --> 00:01:11,109\nEarly auditory areas followed the sounds,\n\n26\n00:01:11,278 --> 00:01:12,350\nwhatever the order.\n\n27\n00:01:12,818 --> 00:01:20,864\nBut regions in frontal and parietal cortex responded reliably only when the whole story came in its meaningful sequence.\n\n28\n00:01:21,034 --> 00:01:22,167\nTwo cautions.\n\n29\n00:01:22,435 --> 00:01:24,737\nA story can carry the wrong cargo.\n\n30\n00:01:25,189 --> 00:01:27,588\nInteresting but irrelevant details,\n\n31\n00:01:27,646 --> 00:01:29,772\nthe vivid anecdote that isn't the point,\n\n32\n00:01:30,023 --> 00:01:32,503\nreduce recall of the main ideas.\n\n33\n00:01:32,816 --> 00:01:36,230\nAnd students may remember the story and forget the concept.\n\n34\n00:01:36,652 --> 00:01:38,372\nThe idea has to be the plot,\n\n35\n00:01:38,392 --> 00:01:39,564\nnot the backdrop.\n\n36\n00:01:39,734 --> 00:01:40,530\nFor teaching.\n\n37\n00:01:40,890 --> 00:01:44,291\nFrame a topic as a problem someone actually faced.\n\n38\n00:01:44,701 --> 00:01:46,725\nWho were they, and what was in the way?\n\n39\n00:01:47,067 --> 00:01:49,215\nMake the key idea the turning point.\n\n40\n00:01:49,668 --> 00:01:51,789\nKeep the details that serve the plot,\n\n41\n00:01:51,809 --> 00:01:52,963\nand cut the rest.\n\n42\n00:01:53,334 --> 00:01:56,619\nAnd hold back the answer long enough for students to wonder.\n\n43\n00:01:56,789 --> 00:01:59,566\nA list asks memory to hold separate pieces.\n\n44\n00:01:59,848 --> 00:02:01,561\nA story gives it a thread.\n\n"
